  "findings": {
    "item": [
      {
        "id": "F1",
        "title": "Reachable class set is 10; inherited classify() recognises 4, inherited probe set observes 2",
        "evidence": "§4, §5 of report; battery8.py output: 'classes it CANNOT observe (6/10): JSON-HARDLINKED, JSON-INSIDE-MEASURED-TREE, JSON-IS-RECORD, JSON-NOT-REGULAR, JSON-UNWRITABLE, USAGE'; 'inherited probe set never observes (8)'. All 10 driven live against committed r40-base bytes (absent path, chmod-0 path, ELOOP symlink, empty record, row escaping --root, --root not a directory, --json==--record, --json under --root, --json FIFO, --json hardlink, --json unwritable parent)."
      },
      {
        "id": "F2",
        "title": "THE FALSIFIER — committed r39 leg produces a FALSE PASS on a wrong mapping, decided by the scratch directory name",
        "evidence": "§7. Same candidate bytes + same committed counter: benign scratch -> 'LEG L4 FAIL missing=RECORD-MALFORMED locked=RECORD-MISSING new_maps=False old_maps=False'; scratch under a RECORD-MISSING dir -> 'LEG L4 PASS missing=RECORD-MISSING locked=RECORD-MISSING new_maps=True old_maps=False'. Candidate is genuinely wrong (runtime still raises RECORD-MALFORMED for ENOENT; its docstring entry lost the trigger). Mechanism: classify() is a substring scan over stdout+stderr and the tool echoes the record path, so a path carrying a class token re-labels the observed class; and the trigger word 'missing' is a substring of the class token 'RECORD-MISSING', so classes['RECORD-MISSING'] satisfies the RECORD-MALFORMED check; both probes then collapse onto one class. FIX VERIFIED: reading the class from the tool's anchored 'REFUSAL <CLASS>:' banner yields FAIL/FAIL under both scratch roots."
      },
      {
        "id": "F3",
        "title": "Base saturation — the r40 base bytes already PASS the joined analyzer over every reachable class, so no base/candidate separation remains on the tool bytes",
