      },
      {
        "id": "F4",
        "title": "Mutant deleting 6 of the 10 docstring entries is invisible to the inherited leg",
        "evidence": "§5. Deleting all JSON-* and USAGE entries (runtime unchanged, still raises all 10) gives the inherited L4 leg new_ok=True, identical to the pristine verdict; the all-scope joined detector FAILs it (NO-ENTRY for all six). The frozen predicate does not say which scope is used, and a containment-only detector that skips absent classes passes the mutant."
      },
      {
        "id": "F5",
        "title": "Trigger vocabulary is unfrozen and flips the verdict on the SAME committed bytes",
        "evidence": "§6. RECORD-MISSING: trigger 'EACCES' -> one-line detects; 'cannot be opened or read' -> one-line misses, joined detects; 'cannot be read' -> neither detects. RECORD-MALFORMED: 'missing' and 'ENOENT' both sit on the first line (one-line detects). Every natural trigger for the two inherited-observed classes sits on the first line, so the frozen predicate's 'r39 one-line analyzer misses it' cannot be satisfied by the real bytes. Also: trigger '--root' for RECORD-OUTSIDE-ROOT also occurs in the JSON-INSIDE-MEASURED-TREE and USAGE entries, so a containment check is satisfied by the wrong entry."
      },
      {
        "id": "F6",
        "title": "The r39 leg shape is unsatisfiable under the frozen base revision",
        "evidence": "§8. Committed counter at --rev 0242085 --base-rev 0242085: 'LEG L4 FAIL missing=RECORD-MALFORMED locked=RECORD-MISSING new_maps=True old_maps=True' -> new_ok and not old_ok is False because the base docstring is already correct. With the r39 base as comparison point instead: 'LEG L4 PASS ... new_maps=True old_maps=False'. The frozen measurement names base 0242085, so that leg shape has no separation left."
      }
