{
  "seat": "R40connector21",
  "role": "connector",
  "axis": "coverage",
  "lane": "/home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin/souji-shokunin",
  "report_path": "/home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin/souji-shokunin/.ufo/waves/round-40/w1/R40connector21.md",
  "base_revision": "0242085909fb6a4ee041c5443d76316ac6d59d4b",
  "named_distinct_edge_case": "The kill-gate misattribution: the r39 counter's `crash` veto reads the FIXTURE's stdout, and the house-style fixture detail format re-prints the TOOL's stdout+stderr into the fixture's own stdout — so a TOOL-side crash is scored as if the FIXTURE had crashed, and the mutant is reported `survived` even though the fixture detected the difference.",
  "distinctness_check": "Compared against all sibling round-40 w1 reports. R40executor39/R40executor51/R40revenger36 take the duplicate-TOTAL multiplicity edge; R40executor63/R40revenger48/R40revenger60 take the invalid-UTF-8 branch and invalid-byte fixture I/O. Item 21 is strictly downstream of R40revenger60: given a fixture that writes invalid bytes correctly in binary and captures correctly, does the counter's kill predicate credit it? Measured answer: no.",
  "verdict": "A concrete falsifier of the frozen C3 predicate is established at the INSTRUMENT level (measurability + mutant admissibility), not of the coverage substance. Both findings are one-line repairs and neither requires changing census/round-40/preregistration.txt.",
  "findings": {
    "item": [
      {
        "id": "F1",
        "severity": "high",
        "title": "Crash-gate misattribution makes the invalid-UTF-8 branch unscoreable under the inherited r39 counter",
        "detail": "C3 requires 'each declared single-defect mutant is killed by the r40 fixture'. The natural mutant for the invalid-UTF-8 branch is decode(\"utf-8\",\"replace\") -> decode(\"utf-8\",\"strict\") (the committed r39 wave already proposed exactly this as N4 in census/round-39/waves/w1/W1MutDriftRules.md:121-127). That mutant makes the tool raise UnicodeDecodeError and die with a traceback. census/round-39/c3-counter.py:101-102 computes crash = \"Traceback (most recent call last)\" in (proc.stdout + proc.stderr) where proc is the FIXTURE process, and the house-style fixture detail format (tests/fixtures/r39-drift-record-rules.py:79-80, same in r38-drift-record.py:79) embeds the tool's captured stdout+stderr into the fixture's own stdout. The tool's traceback therefore lands in the fixture's stdout, crash flips to True, and the kill is vetoed.",
        "measured": "The fixture DID detect the difference — SUMMARY cases=6 pass=4 fail=2, exit 1, exactly the A5 kill conditions — and the counter still reported killed=no. Decisive isolation: two fixtures identical except for traceback redaction, same tool/mutant/case; house-style gives strict-mutant killed=False crash=True, traceback-scrubbed gives killed=True crash=False. Pristine passes both variants identically (rc=0 fails=0 crash=False), so the flip is attributable to the mutant.",
        "latent_in_r39": "All four r39 receipts read crash=False (census/round-39/records/c3-after.txt:3-6), so the defect never fired in r39. It becomes reachable for the first time on the invalid-UTF-8 branch — one of the three branches C3 newly names.",
        "a5_intent_preserved": "Isolated the two crash sources: a fixture raising in its OWN frames is correctly killed=False (A5 intent preserved), and a fixture reprinting the TOOL traceback is also killed=False (the misattribution). The two are textually indistinguishable to the current gate."
      },
      {
        "id": "F2",
        "severity": "medium",
        "title": "Mutant admissibility constraint: one natural multiple-TOTAL mutant is already killed by the r39 base fixture",
        "detail": "C3 also requires 'all mutants survive the r39 base fixture' (base_killed=0). The mutant that makes the parser require the TOTAL row to come AFTER all REPO rows is already killed by both committed base fixtures (r39: cases=7 pass=5 fail=2; r38: cases=10 pass=5 fail=5). It is therefore INADMISSIBLE as an r40 C3 mutant: declaring it fails the predicate on its own control clause, and that failure is a battery defect, not a coverage gap. The r39 base fixture's own total-before-rows case already accepts a TOTAL row before the REPO rows.",
        "measured": "Admissibility matrix (scored with the committed counter's own score()): MT-duplicate-TOTAL-refused killed=True, r39:killed=False r38:killed=False (admissible); MT-total-first-wins killed=True, both bases alive (admissible); MT-total-must-be-last killed=True but r39:killed=True r38:killed=True (INADMISSIBLE); MT-skip-unrecorded-inverted killed=True, both bases alive (admissible); MT-decode-ignore killed=True, both bases alive (admissible, and the practical workaround for F1); MT-decode-strict killed=False crash=True (F1 victim, inadmissible)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "pristine_behaviour_measured": {
    "item": [
      {
        "invalid_utf8": "The pristine tool TOLERATES invalid UTF-8 (decode \"replace\" substitutes U+FFFD); it does NOT refuse. A fixture asserting 'invalid UTF-8 -> RECORD-MALFORMED' would FAIL on the pristine candidate and break C3's pristine-pass clause. Placement matters: invalid byte in a REPO name field -> rc=1 with DRIFT ABSENT AND DRIFT UNRECORDED (the corrupted name no longer matches the recorded name, so the real alpha becomes unrecorded — two drift rows, not one); alone on a structural line -> rc=2 RECORD-MALFORMED; inside a COMMENT line -> rc=0 fully tolerated; inside TOTAL numbers -> rc=2. The tool's docstring names NO refusal class for a decoding error."
      },
      {
        "skip_unrecorded": "Suppresses more than the UNRECORDED row: it also suppresses the UNREACHABLE row for an unrecorded-but-broken repo, and moves the unreachable= summary counter from 1 to 0 (drift_rows=2 -> drift_rows=0). A case asserting only 'no UNRECORDED row' leaves two further observables unpinned."
      },
      {
        "duplicate_total": "Last TOTAL wins; the EARLIER row is never validated. A bogus first TOTAL followed by a good second TOTAL is ACCEPTED (rc=0). total is overwritten on each match (tools/souji-worktree-drift.py:162-165) and only the final value is checked (:169-178)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "fixture_design_landmine": "The r39 fixture builds ONE shared fleet that every case reuses, and its 'good' case asserts rc=0 on it. Adding an unrecorded repo to that shared fleet flips every other case's exit code from 0 to 1 (DRIFT UNRECORDED gamma), silently breaking pristine. The r40 fixture must use a SEPARATE fleet for the --skip-unrecorded case (my scratch fixture does).",
  "inherited_counter_constraints": {
    "item": [
      "cases >= 6 required for pristine_ok (census/round-39/c3-counter.py:161)",
      "mutant anchor must occur exactly once (c3-counter.py:161-166); decode(\"utf-8\", \"replace\") occurs 4 times in the tool, so the full line at :147 (count 1) is required",
      "--fixture/--base-fixture are read from the filesystem while the tool is read from the revision via git show; for C3 the fixture and tool must come from the same candidate revision",
      "preregistration's 'explicit scratch paths, no hardcoded /tmp' is already satisfied by --scratch + tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=...)"
    ]
  },
  "proposed_move": {
    "proposed_move": [
      {
        "item": [
          {
            "priority": "1",
            "change": "Separate the two crash sources in the r40 counter's kill gate: treat a traceback in the FIXTURE's own frames as the A5 veto, and recognise a TOOL-side traceback as a KILL (a tool that dies is precisely the behavioural difference the fixture observed).",
            "measured_effect": "Turns MT-decode-strict from killed=no to killed=yes (§2.1). Smallest alternative needing no counter change: the r40 fixture redacts the tool's traceback from its own failure detail before printing (the traceback-scrubbed variant) — also measured killed=True.",
            "preserves": "§2.2 shows the fixture-self-crash case stays killed=False, so A5's intent is intact either way."
          },
          {
            "priority": "2",
            "change": "Declare only admissible mutants: for multiple-TOTAL use duplicate-TOTAL-refused or first-TOTAL-wins (not total-must-be-last); for invalid-UTF-8 use a non-crashing decode-policy mutant such as replace -> ignore, or the strict mutant after priority-1 repair; for --skip-unrecorded use an inverted-flag or dropped-UNRECORDED-row mutant.",
            "measured_effect": "All four admissible shapes measured killed=True with both base fixtures alive."
          },
          {
            "priority": "3",
            "change": "Use a separate fleet for the --skip-unrecorded case and assert the measured pristine expectations (tolerate invalid UTF-8; --skip-unrecorded also drops the unrecorded UNREACHABLE row and zeroes unreachable=).",
            "measured_effect": "Prevents the shared-fleet landmine and the pristine-failure trap."
          },
          {
            "priority": "4",
            "change": "Pin the counter's own constraints in the r40 fixture/counter header: cases >= 6, anchor-count == 1, the SUMMARY cases=/pass=/fail= line, and the base-fixture survival clause."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "axis_effect": "Improves C3 coverage measurability (the invalid-UTF-8 branch moves from unscoreable to scoreable) and C3 operability (the counter's kill predicate stops mis-attributing a tool crash to the fixture). Harms no other axis: C1 calibration and C2 operability are untouched, and no frozen claim is edited."
      }
    ],
    "verification": {
      "performed": {
        "item": [
          "Decisive F1 isolation via two fixtures differing only in traceback redaction (house-style killed=False crash=True vs scrubbed killed=True crash=False; pristine rc=0 fails=0 crash=False in both)",
